  "result": "I wrote the revised record for LVMC_11_30_060_A to `record.jsonl`. It hasn't been validated or run through the gate yet. The harness and coder do that next.\n\n**Cause:** the gate returned VACUOUS on obligation leaf 7, the pedestrian's speed test. The trigger already required the pedestrian to be moving across a carrier at the judged instant. So wherever the rule applied, the speed was already above `p.halt_speed_tolerance`, and the speed test could never come out false. A pedestrian who stopped mid-crossing fell outside the rule, so they could never count as compliant for having stopped. The old `branch_1` scenario depended on exactly that case.\n\n**Fix (the coder's option b, using `since` instead of a window):** I changed what \"crossing a roadway\" (f2) means and kept the same atoms and concept. The pedestrian now counts as crossing if they occupy the roadway and have stayed on it since a moment when they were moving across a lane or the roadway itself. That moment can be now. This uses the past operator `since`, so no new window parameter is needed.\n- A pedestrian who halts mid-crossing is still in the rule. The obligation's speed test now separates holding back from walking on, which matches the record's reading of \"shall yield\".\n- A pedestrian who only walks along the roadway never counts as crossing.\n- f4 keeps `kin.speed` and `rel.contact`, so it still matches how the index maps \"shall yield\".\n\n**Other changes:**\n- **Obligation:** it now re-selects the roadway with the applicability's full selection, including the no-`pedestrians_prohibited`-sign exception (f8). Before, it left that exception out.\n- **Scenarios:**\n  - `branch_1` (compliant) is rewritten: the pedestrian is at rest in the lane while a car passes about 2 m from their centre. A conflict is projected, so this exercises the speed test.\n  - `branch_2` (not applicable) is new: a pedestrian walking along the roadway.\n  - `branch_3` (violated) is new: a pedestrian halts in the lane, then walks into a projected conflict with a car.\n- **Parameters:** no new ones. The four the record uses are already in the catalogue, so I dropped the old parameter proposals.\n\nHow Core evaluates `since` at the very start of a recording (unknown or false) is left to the coder to handle.",
